{"issues":[{"id":"local-1","kind":"translation","category":"authorVoice","severity":3,"sourceQuote":"If Code is Communication, please `export` like you fucking mean it. 💞","targetQuote":"Si el código es comunicación, por favor `export`a como si en verdad te importara. 💞","replacement":"Si el código es comunicación, por favor, haz `export` con toda la puta intención. 💞","rationale":"The translation cuts the profanity from the article's final punchline, and it also changes the meaning. 'Mean it' is about intent: export on purpose. 'Te importara' means caring. The whole argument is about signaling intent, so the swap matters. The replacement keeps both the swear word and the intent. 'Haz `export`' also avoids the hybrid '`export`a'. Tradeoff: some readers may find it crude, but the source chose this register on purpose, and nothing in the task calls for softening it. Saying 'puta' works as an intensifier across varieties is my own usage judgment, not a checked source.","alternative":"Si el código es comunicación, por favor, exporta como si lo dijeras en serio, joder. 💞 (the 'joder' version leans toward Spain)","confidence":0.72,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"Broad Spanish-speaking developers; 'joder' in the alternative leans Peninsular","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source uses 'fucking mean it' in the conclusion, after the claim that 'Code is Communication' and the line about sending a signal about how to use a thing."}]}, {"id":"local-2","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Have we missed the point of `export`ing in the first place?","targetQuote":"¿Acaso hemos perdido el punto de `export`ar en primer lugar?","replacement":"¿No será que hemos perdido de vista para qué servía `export` desde el principio?","rationale":"'Perder el punto' and 'en primer lugar' (meaning 'to begin with') are word-for-word calques of the English. '`export`ar' also stitches a Spanish verb ending onto a code keyword. The replacement keeps the rhetorical question and the keyword in backticks.","alternative":"¿Se nos ha olvidado para qué sirve `export` en realidad?","confidence":0.8,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source sentence asks about the underlying purpose of exporting."}]}, {"id":"local-3","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Neat feature. Not the point.","targetQuote":"Función interesante. No es el punto.","replacement":"Función interesante. No viene al caso.","rationale":"'No es el punto' is a calque of 'not the point'. 'No viene al caso' is the idiomatic Spanish and keeps the curt rhythm.","alternative":"Muy bonito. Pero no es de lo que hablamos.","confidence":0.8,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source is a short dismissive aside."}]}, {"id":"local-4","kind":"translation","category":"readability","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"> We are sending a signal to `import`ers _how to use a thing._","targetQuote":"> Le estamos enviando una señal a los `import`ers _sobre cómo usar algo._","replacement":"> Le estamos enviando una señal a quien hace `import` _sobre cómo usar algo._","rationale":"'`import`ers' carries an English suffix into Spanish prose and reads like a leftover. A relative clause keeps the keyword in backticks and reads naturally. The italics are kept.","alternative":"> Les estamos diciendo a quienes importan el módulo _cómo usar algo._","confidence":0.75,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source's 'importers' just means the consumers of the module."}]}, {"id":"local-5","kind":"translation","category":"faithfulness","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"A `named export` says it’s “definitely **_A THING!_**”","targetQuote":"Una `exportación nombrada` dice que es \"definitivamente **_UNA COSA_**.\"","replacement":"Un `named export` dice que es \"definitivamente **_¡UNA COSA!_**\".","rationale":"Two problems. First, the translation put a Spanish phrase inside code formatting. Either keep the English term in backticks, as the parallel `export default` bullet does, or drop the backticks. Second, the exclamation mark in 'A THING!' was lost, which flattens the joke. The period also belongs outside the closing quote in Spanish.","alternative":"Una exportación nombrada (`named export`) dice que es \"definitivamente **_¡UNA COSA!_**\".","confidence":0.7,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"Spanish technical prose","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source has the term in backticks and 'A THING!' with an exclamation mark."},{"referenceId":"mdn-export-es","explanation":"Spanish MDN distinguishes named and default exports. Both 'exportación nombrada' and the English term are understood."}]}, {"id":"local-6","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Also raises some questions, “got any other buddies there?“ Follow up, “Are they invited or required?”","targetQuote":"También plantea algunas preguntas, \"¿tienes otros compañeros por ahí?\" Seguimiento: \"¿Están invitados o son obligatorios?\"","replacement":"También plantea algunas preguntas: \"¿Tienes más compañeros por ahí?\". Y la siguiente: \"¿Están invitados o son obligatorios?\"","rationale":"'Seguimiento:' is a noun-for-noun rendering of 'Follow up'. It sounds like a meeting agenda, not a follow-up question. The replacement also fixes the colon and the capital letter after the opening question mark.","alternative":"…Y, de paso: \"¿Están invitados o son obligatorios?\"","confidence":0.78,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"In the source, 'Follow up' introduces a second, related question."}]}, {"id":"local-7","kind":"translation","category":"faithfulness","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Now tell me, how do I hold your contraption again? No author intent.","targetQuote":"Ahora dime, ¿cómo sostengo tu invento otra vez? Sin intención del autor.","replacement":"A ver, dime otra vez: ¿por dónde se agarra tu invento? No transmite ninguna intención del autor.","rationale":"'Sin intención del autor' can be misread as 'done unintentionally by the author'. The point is that the pattern tells the reader nothing about what the author intended. 'Por dónde se agarra' keeps the knife-handle joke better than 'cómo sostengo'.","alternative":"Ahora dime, ¿cómo se sujeta tu invento? No comunica la intención del autor.","confidence":0.7,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The surrounding thesis is that exports signal the author's intent."}]}, {"id":"local-8","kind":"translation","category":"readability","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Incorrect (any more). Configure/update your tools.","targetQuote":"Incorrecto (ya no más). Configura/actualiza tus herramientas.","replacement":"Ya no es cierto. Configura o actualiza tus herramientas.","rationale":"'Incorrecto (ya no más)' reads backwards: it seems to say it is no longer incorrect. The source means 'no longer true'.","alternative":"Incorrecto (a día de hoy). Configura o actualiza tus herramientas.","confidence":0.75,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The next bullet says support has existed for 3+ years, which confirms the meaning is 'no longer true'."}]}, {"id":"local-9","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":1,"sourceQuote":"“Presenting ONE function w/ Single Purpose!”","targetQuote":"\"¡Presentando UNA función con un solo propósito!\"","replacement":"\"¡Les presento UNA función con un único propósito!\"","rationale":"A bare gerund as an announcement ('Presentando…') is an anglicism. A presenter's voice fits the showman tone better.","alternative":"\"¡Con ustedes, UNA función con un único propósito!\"","confidence":0.6,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source is a showman-style announcement."}]}, {"id":"local-10","kind":"translation","category":"faithfulness","severity":1,"sourceQuote":"“This is **_THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT_** thing.”","targetQuote":"\"Esta es **_LA COSA MÁS IMPORTANTE_**.\"","replacement":"\"Esta es **_LA COSA MÁS IMPORTANTE DE TODAS_**\".","rationale":"'SINGLE' adds emphasis on uniqueness that the translation drops. 'DE TODAS' brings back the emphatic, one-of-a-kind sense. Spanish punctuation also puts the period after the closing quote.","alternative":"\"Esto es **_LO MÁS IMPORTANTE, Y PUNTO_**\".","confidence":0.55,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source emphasizes 'THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT'."}]}, {"id":"local-11","kind":"translation","category":"readability","severity":1,"sourceQuote":"Arg #3: Named exports have better IDE import or renaming support.","targetQuote":"Por supuesto, puedes combinar ambos o usar diferentes enfoques","replacement":"Por supuesto, puedes combinar ambas o usar diferentes enfoques","rationale":"'Ambos' refers to the two kinds of export, and 'exportación' is feminine. The code comment already uses '¡Puedes usar alias con ambas!', so 'ambas' also keeps the text consistent. Note: the sourceQuote field shows the wrong excerpt; the right one is 'Of course you can combine both'.","alternative":"Por supuesto, puedes combinar las dos","confidence":0.6,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"'Combine both' refers to the two export types listed just before."}]}, {"id":"local-12","kind":"translation","category":"mdxIntegrity","severity":1,"sourceQuote":"---","targetQuote":"sourceHash: \"438740274604\"","replacement":"sourceHash: \"438740274604\"","rationale":"I'm leaving the value unchanged, but it doesn't look like the actual source hash in the history (7d9ec399…). It may be a hash from a different scheme or an old one. Provenance metadata is controlled, so the coordinator should check it; I won't invent a value.","alternative":"","confidence":0.35,"requiresExternalEvidence":true,"audienceScope":"Repository tooling","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"list_translation_history shows actual sourceHash 7d9ec3992b22… and declaredSourceHash 438740274604 for the current revision."}]}],"strengths":["The frontmatter follows the repository rules: image paths use ../, inherited date is left out, and the controlled metadata is kept.","The summary anchor was correctly changed to #resumen so it points to the localized '## Resumen' heading.","The code blocks, URLs, identifiers and the source typo './kinves.js' are unchanged. Only the code comments are translated, and accurately.","The core technical claims are faithful: aliasing, the export default/const restriction, and support for classes. Terminology matches Spanish MDN ('exportación nombrada' / 'por defecto').","The table keeps the same rows, the checkmark pattern and the MDX <section> wrapper.","'Odio ser quien te lo diga, ¡pero espera a ver lo que pueden hacer las variables!' keeps the author's sarcasm naturally."],"sourceConcerns":["The Summary table's checkmarks don't match its Pattern text. Row 3 says 'Default export + multiple private un-exported functions' but marks Named ✅. Row 4 says 'Multiple named exports' but marks Named ❌. The translation keeps these as written; the author should review.","'./kinves.js' looks like a typo for 'knives'. It's inside code, so it was correctly kept as is.","'AirBnb' is usually written 'Airbnb'. This is a minor source stylization and is kept."]}
